From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work6_pr_fixes_use <>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 12:51:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] os/mm: Bound the use-after-free hex dump

mm_uaf_verify() dumped the whole chunk on detection. The most likely
victim of a stray write is the large top of heap free chunk, so the
report could emit one line per 32 bytes of the entire remaining heap.
On a serial console that is thousands of lines with the memory manager
semaphore held, which trips the watchdog. In report only mode this
happened on a path that was meant to be recoverable.

Cap the dump with the new CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_DUMP_SIZE, default 256
bytes. A chunk smaller than the cap is still dumped in full, so the
bytes on either side of the corruption stay visible; only oversized
chunks are truncated. The poisoned window is always included even when
the cap is smaller than the window, so the evidence is never cut out of
the report.
---
 os/Kconfig.debug       | 12 ++++++++++++
 os/mm/mm_heap/mm_uaf.c | 20 +++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 31 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/os/Kconfig.debug b/os/Kconfig.debug
index 17b9fe4a..e5e37933 100644
--- a/os/Kconfig.debug
+++ b/os/Kconfig.debug
@@ -811,6 +811,18 @@ config DEBUG_MM_UAF_POISON_SIZE
 		covered. Larger values detect more corruption but slow down every
 		free and every malloc.
 
+config DEBUG_MM_UAF_DUMP_SIZE
+	int "Maximum bytes of a corrupted chunk to hex dump"
+	default 256
+	---help---
+		Upper bound on the hex dump printed when a corrupted chunk is found.
+		A chunk smaller than this is dumped in full, so that the bytes around
+		the corruption stay visible. A larger chunk is truncated to this many
+		bytes. Without the bound a corrupted top of heap chunk dumps the whole
+		remaining heap, one line per 32 bytes, which is slow enough on a serial
+		console to trip the watchdog. The poisoned window is always dumped even
+		if this value is smaller than the window itself.
+
 config DEBUG_MM_UAF_PANIC
 	bool "Panic when use-after-free is detected"
 	default n
diff --git a/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_uaf.c b/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_uaf.c
index a5706630..8eb7f94b 100644
--- a/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_uaf.c
+++ b/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_uaf.c
@@ -129,6 +129,7 @@ void mm_uaf_verify(struct mm_freenode_s *node)
 {
 	uint32_t *poison;
 	size_t nwords;
+	size_t dumpsize;
 	size_t i;
 
 	nwords = mm_uaf_area(node, &poison);
@@ -144,10 +145,27 @@ void mm_uaf_verify(struct mm_freenode_s *node)
 		return;
 	}
 
+	/* Decide how much of the chunk to dump. A small chunk is shown in full so
+	 * that the bytes around the corruption stay visible, but the dump is never
+	 * longer than CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_DUMP_SIZE. Without that cap a corrupted
+	 * top of heap chunk emits one line per 32 bytes of the whole remaining
+	 * heap, which takes long enough to trip the watchdog. The verified window
+	 * is always included, even when the cap is smaller than the window.
+	 */
+
+	dumpsize = MM_ALIGN_UP(SIZEOF_MM_FREENODE) + nwords * sizeof(uint32_t);
+	if (dumpsize < CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_DUMP_SIZE) {
+		dumpsize = CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_DUMP_SIZE;
+	}
+
+	if (dumpsize > node->size) {
+		dumpsize = node->size;
+	}
+
 	heap_dbg("#########################################################################################\n");
 	mfdbg("ERROR: Use after free detected.\n");
 	mm_dump_node((struct mm_allocnode_s *)node, "FREE NODE");
-	mm_dump_heap_region((uint32_t)node, (uint32_t)node + node->size);
+	mm_dump_heap_region((uint32_t)node, (uint32_t)node + dumpsize);
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_PANIC
 	PANIC();
